MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I turns out that the current release R 2.15.2 from http://cran.r-project.org/bin/macosx/ has 64-bit support but it just doesn't switch the default from 32-bit to 64-bit on Mac OS X 10.8 (Mountain Lion). The next release will.

The better solution is to install R 2.15.3 (nightly build) from http://r.research.att.com/ on top of R 2.15.2 from http://cran.r-project.org/bin/macosx/ so that both R and Rscript work in 64-bit mode.

Now RHadoop works perfectly with R 2.15.3 on Mac OS X 10.8 and hadoop-1.0.4 in pseudo-distributed mode.

-Alton

On Saturday, February 23, 2013 2:33:18 AM UTC-8, Alton Ing wrote:
> Hi Antonio (and other Mac OS X users)
>
> To get Rscript running in 64-bit mode and RHadoop working locally in pseudo-distributed mode on your 64-bit Mac OS X (Mountain Lion):
>
> sudo ln -s /Library/Frameworks/R.framework/Resources/bin/Rscript /usr/bin/Rscript32
> sudo rm /usr/bin/Rscript
> sudo echo '/Library/Frameworks/R.framework/Resources/bin/Rscript --arch=x86_64 "$@"' > /usr/local/bin/Rscript64
> sudo cp /usr/local/bin/Rscript64 /usr/bin/Rscript
> sudo chown root:admin /usr/bin/Rscript
> sudo chmod 775 /usr/bin/Rscript
>
> To confirm Rscript (and RHadoop) runs in 64-bit mode
> R64 # to start R in 64-bit mode
> library(rmr2)
> from.dfs(mapreduce(input=to.dfs(1),map=function(.,v)sessionInfo()$platform)) # expect "x86_64-apple-darwin9.8.0/x86_64 (64-bit)"
>
> # another test
> groups = rbinom(32, n = 50, prob = 0.4)
> tapply(groups, groups, length)
> from.dfs( mapreduce(input = to.dfs(groups), map = function(., v) keyval(v, 1), reduce = function(k, vv) keyval(k, length(vv)))) # expect same as tapply
>
> If your old scripts encounter problems try using /usr/bin/Rscript32 instead. Otherwise, you can revert /usr/bin/Rscript back using:
> sudo rm /usr/bin/Rscript
> sudo ln -s /Library/Frameworks/R.framework/Resources/bin/Rscript /usr/bin/Rscript
>
> -Alton
>
